    pr_ref:  # fbf4
    description: "Per-edge `confidence` values are assigned by each emitter as a hardcoded constant (or one of a small fixed set of constants) rather than computed from the inference evidence used to produce the edge. The schema documents `confidence` as evidence-derived, but production code emits constant-per-bucket values across substantially all edges. Only one emitter (`type-hierarchy`) produces non-rounded computed values; every other emitter ships a flat hardcoded value per origin or per (edge-type, evidence-type) tuple.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) Most linkers emit a single hardcoded `confidence` value across all their edges.** `decorator-dispatch` always emits `0.7` (292 edges, 1 distinct value); `dependency-linker` always emits `0.9` (26 edges, 1 value); `enclosure-linker` always emits `0.9` (119 edges, 1 value); `pyffi-linker` always emits `0.75`; `database-query-linker` always emits `0.85`; `json` always emits `0.95`. Only `type-hierarchy` uses real variance (12 unique values across 324 edges). The `confidence` field exists, is 100% populated, but doesn't reflect per-edge uncertainty — it's just a per-linker label. Root cause: linkers were ported with hardcoded confidence constants. No calibration step computes per-edge confidence based on resolution method, ambiguity, or evidence quality.\n\n**(2) The Python emitter hardcodes 5 confidence values across edge-type buckets with no apparent semantic axis distinguishing values within a bucket.** Five distinct hardcoded values: `0.85` (calls=36663, references=2877, module_attr_ref=1323), `0.95` (imports=10457, instantiates=7803, decorated_by=270, extends=147), `0.4` (calls=16080), `0.5` (calls=7714, decorated_by=129), `0.8` (references=1322). `type=calls` splits `0.85` vs `0.4` vs `0.5` across 60,567 edges — same edge type, three different confidence values, no apparent semantic rule distinguishing the buckets. Python analyzer code hardcodes confidence per (edge-type, evidence-type) tuple; no calibration loop and no input-dependent variability.\n\n### Invariant\n\n`Confidence` should be computed from the inference evidence — e.g., type-resolution success rate, ambiguity of dispatch resolution, evidence-type tier, or call-target uniqueness. Hardcoded values should be the fallback when evidence-derived computation is not available, and the fallback should be documented as such in the schema (or surfaced via a `confidence_source` field). The schema's \"evidence-derived\" framing should match what production code produces.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects every edge with a `confidence` value (substantially all edges in the behavior map). The 5 hardcoded Python values cover the great majority of records; per-linker hardcoded values cover the linker subset.\n\n### Exposure\n\nBehavior-map per-edge `confidence` field across all emitters.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers using `confidence` to rank or filter edges get rankings driven by the emitter's per-bucket constant choice, not by actual edge quality. Comparing edges across emitters by `confidence` is meaningless — a `0.85` from the Python analyzer's `calls` bucket and a `0.85` from the `database-query-linker` mean entirely different things. Ranking by confidence in a sketch output or slice produces an ordering that reflects emitter conventions rather than per-edge inference quality.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- For the Python analyzer (the largest source): define a calibration loop that computes per-edge confidence from evidence quality — type-resolution success rate, call-target uniqueness, fallback-to-string-match. Replace the (edge-type, evidence-type) → constant lookup.\n- For each linker: define a per-linker confidence rule that varies with the inference path. E.g., `decorator-dispatch` could emit higher confidence when the decorator target is uniquely resolved vs ambiguous.\n- Document the fallback: when no evidence-derived computation is available, emit a documented sentinel value (e.g., `confidence=0.5` with a `confidence_source: hardcoded_fallback` flag) so consumers can filter out non-calibrated edges.\n- Add a property test that asserts no single emitter produces only one `confidence` value across >100 edges (catches the regression where calibration is missed).\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-poriv, INV-dinuh — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # fbf4
    fields:  # fbf4
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-b4686b"  # fbf4
      statement: "Confidence should be computed from the inference evidence — for example, type-resolution success rate, ambiguity of dispatch resolution, or evidence-type tier. Hardcoded values should be the fallback when evidence-derived computation is not available, and the fallback should be documented."  # fbf4
      root_cause: "Per-edge confidence values are assigned by each emitter as a hardcoded constant (or one of a small fixed set of constants) rather than computed from the inference evidence used to produce the edge. One language emitter uses five hardcoded values across its edge-type buckets, with no documented semantic axis distinguishing the values within a single bucket — for example, the same edge type splits between two different confidence values with no apparent rule. Across the broader emitter set, every emitter except one ships a flat hardcoded value per origin, with only one emitter producing computed non-rounded values from actual inference quality. The schema documents confidence as evidence-derived but the production code is constant-per-bucket."  # fbf4
